# Generated from: specs/14-establish-production-readiness-and-anonymous-abuse-controls/requirements.md
Feature: Establish production readiness and anonymous abuse controls
  As a group maintainer
  I want deployment health, schema changes, recovery, and public endpoints to fail safely
  So that a small volunteer organization can operate the service without hidden infrastructure risk

  @SCN001
  Scenario: PostgreSQL readiness controls deployment admission
    Given Railway Infrastructure as Code configures the Web health check as /health/ready
    And process liveness remains available at /health
    When PostgreSQL is unavailable to a new Web deployment
    Then /health/ready is unhealthy
    And /health still reports the serving process as live
    And Railway does not admit the deployment

  @SCN002
  Scenario: A release applies migrations before serving
    Given a release contains a pending EF Core migration
    And Railway's Web service pre-deploy command is dotnet VolunteerCoordinator.Web.dll --migrate-only
    When Railway deploys the release
    Then one non-serving process applies the migration and exits successfully
    And the normal Web process does not apply migrations
    And Railway admits the Web process only after PostgreSQL readiness succeeds

  @SCN003
  Scenario Outline: Excess anonymous traffic is rejected without state change
    Given one client address has exhausted the <tier> limit
    And matching valid and invalid route identifiers exist
    When that client sends another valid request and another invalid request
    Then both responses are HTTP 429
    And both responses contain the same generic retry text and retry guidance
    And neither request executes a page handler or changes workflow state

    Examples:
      | tier                       |
      | request mutation           |
      | private-token read         |
      | assignment-action mutation|

  @SCN004
  Scenario: A daily backup restores authoritative workflow state
    Given the selected production backup is no more than 24 hours old
    And the operator creates a new isolated PostgreSQL target
    When the operator follows the recovery runbook
    Then EF migration history is restored
    And schedule, volunteer, request, assignment, action-token metadata, notification, and audit state pass integrity checks
    And representative coordinator reads succeed against only the isolated target
    And production data is not overwritten by the drill

  @SCN005
  Scenario: A second coordinator preserves operational continuity
    Given two distinct verified OIDC identities are listed in the production coordinator allowlist
    When each identity signs in independently
    Then each identity can operate the existing schedule
    And no credentials are shared
    And the outgoing identity is removed only after the incoming identity succeeds
